urces  which did not rely on each other for  their information then obviously neither  of them made it up  scholars then tried to figure out what  about these gospels can be independent  do matthew and  luke have a source other than mark for  example for their stories is john  dependent on matthew and mark and luke  or what kind of independent sources  where people were not relying on each  other and if you have stories that are  independently attested especially if  they're independently attested in lots  of sources and obviously those stories  are more likely to be things that  happened because no one of these sources  could have made it up since independent  sources know about it  moreover  another criterion if a story told by a  christian source promotes a particular  point of view  or a perspective  or a belief that the author himself  obviously held dear and wanted to  emphasize  it's hard to know if that author has  altered the historical record in order  to make it support his belief  but if you have a source that tells a  story that seems to run  counter  to what the author otherwise would like  to affirm and believe  well he obviously didn't make that one  up  he's not telling it because he's trying  to promote his own agenda  but simply because he heard it happened  if you have stories that cut across the  gospel's own agendas  and if you find these in multiple  independent sources there's a much  greater probability that these  particular stories are historical  what scholars do of course is they go  through the gospels story by story  sentence by sentence almost word by word  to decide  which ones are independently attested  and which ones are not promoting a  particular agenda that and and if you  can show that  then that story that word that sends is  more likely historically accurate  also and finally  if there's a story that does not make  any sense  in its alleged historical context  you know it can't be right  for example in modern times suppose  you're reading a story about a chef  in the 1920s  who's upset with her microwave  [Music]  you know this can't be a historical  story there were michael waves 1920  if there are stories about jesus that  make no sense from what we know about  first century israel for example from  the writings of josephus and other  sources if they really cannot be located  in the first century of israel they  almost certainly cannot be accepted as  reliable  those are the kind of criteria that  scholars use and the vast majority of  scholars using those criteria agree on  certain pieces of factual information  about jesus  these are some of the facts that i think  we know  jesus was born and raised in the jewish  household  and was thoroughly jewish in every way  from the beginning of his life to the  end  jesus was from a small hamlet  in northern israel called nazareth  we know about nazareth from archaeology  we don't know it from any literary  sources from the time it's never  mentioned in the old testament it's not  mentioned in josephus it's not mentioned  in jewish sources of the day because it  was so small and insignificant  but archaeologists have dug it up and  they've shown that in fact it was small  and insignificant  it was occupied entirely by lower class  farmers and day laborers who are living  a hand-to-mouth existence they've dug it  up  and  it maybe had 200 300 people living in it  it was not a significant place there  were no public buildings in it no  schools no synagogue nothing it's just a  group of houses a hamlet  we don't know anything for certain about  jesus upbringing  although there are obviously good  reasons for thinking he always was or at  least became  seriously committed to the religious  traditions of israel  jesus native language was aramaic  it's a language related to hebrew  it's debated whether jesus would have  known other languages  greek was the lingua franca of the  educated elite throughout the roman  empire but in small rural hamlets and  towns in galilee and other places  throughout the empire they didn't speak  greek  it's possible jesus spoke greek but  the evidence seems to be against it and  he almost certainly didn't know latin  he probably spoke arabic  jesus almost certainly worked impossibly  starting out as a boy  doing what his father did  which may have involved some kind of  carpentry work there's only one verse in  the new testament that says  jesus was a carpenter  mark chapter 6 verse 3  the greek word that's used there is tech  tone jesus was a tech tone  and a tech tone really means somebody  who works with their hands and so it  could be a stonesmith or a metalsmith or  somebody works with wood if he did work  with wood it wouldn't have been fine  cabinetry he would have been making  gates and yokes and other rough wood  products  as an adult jesus appears to have left  home to engage in an itinerant preaching  ministry  we don't know his age at the time  according to the gospel of luke he was  about 30  but i'm not sure how luke would know  this luke was writing 50 years later  the other gospels don't say how long did  his ministry last  well if you read the gospel mark it  seems like it lasts a few months  john indicates it must have lasted over  two years between two and three years  because he indicates jesus celebrated  three different passover festivals  so those are those are basic things we  know about jesus we also know the kind  of views that jesus brought to his  teaching  i've already said the jews at the time  had wide a wide range of beliefs and  practices  one of the dominant jewish belief  systems at the time  is a belief system that scholars call  apocalypticism it comes from the greek  word apocalypsis which means a  disclosing or a revealing  this was a widespread view  jewish apocalypticists who believed that  god had disclosed or revealed that the  end of history was coming had a certain  way of expressing their views  in their view the world that they live  in was controlled by forces of evil  why is this world such a mess if god has  controlled it if god created this if  god's in charge why is there so much  poverty and misery and suffering  well different people have different  answers the apocalyptis has said  that god had allowed the forces of evil  to take over  but  he is soon going to intervene in history  to destroy those forces of evil and  bring in a good kingdom here on earth  god would intervene  and he would annihilate the powers  aligned against him and everybody who  sided with them  but  those who are on the side of god  will be brought into a paradise  a return to the garden of eden  everyone who had been faithful to god  will return to the place that god had  originally designed for humans  this view as i said was supported by a  wide swath of jews in jesus time in the  jewish homeland including pharisees  and the members of the dead sea scroll  community the essenes john the baptist  the latter of whom  jesus associated with before beginning  his own ministry jesus starts out  associating with an apocalypticist john  the baptist  that is the first recorded event in  jesus life in the gospels the first  thing jesus does before his ministry is  he gets baptized by john the baptist  an apocalyptic preacher  urging jews to repent of their sins in  light of the judgment of god that was  soon to arrive  jesus aligned himself with john at the  outset of his ministry  that shows where jesus own ministry was  heading  he too was to proclaim the imminent  arrival of the kingdom of god and the  need to repent in preparation for it  jesus proclamation can be summarized in  his first recorded words our earliest  gospel was mark  and the first thing jesus says in mark  is found in mark chapter 1 verse 15  after jesus is baptized he goes through  his temptation and then he arrives  among his people and he proclaims  the time has been fulfilled  the kingdom of god  is at hand  repent  and believe the good news  this is an apocalyptic message  the time has been fulfilled as an  apocalyptic idea that the forces of evil  have a certain amount of time allotted  to them and the time is up  the kingdom of god  where there'll be a utopia on earth  which god will rule not these crummy  governments in charge now is at hand  it's near it's almost here  you need to get ready for it repent and  turn back to god  so that you will enter into god's  kingdom  jesus taught that a judgment day was  imminent  the kingdom of god for jesus was not  heaven when you die jesus did not think  that you died and your soul went to  heaven jesus like other jews in his time  thought that the kingdom of god was an  actual kingdom  here on earth  run by god through his messiah the  future leader of israel  jesus taught  that this kingdom of god would be  brought by a kind of heavenly cosmic  judge  who would come from heaven in order to  judge the earth  and he would rid this world of the evil  governments that are currently  running the world now  this cosmic judge is going to destroy  those governments  and the representatives who on their  representatives and everyone who sides  with them  only god's true followers would enter  into this coming kingdom  and jesus thought this utopian kingdom  was coming very soon  people need to prepare for it  how soon is it coming  jesus tells his disciples  some of you standing here will not taste  death before they see that the kingdom  of god has come in power  he's talking to his disciples you won't  some of you won't die before you see it  come  this generation  will not pass away before all these  things take place says jesus  people need to prepare  for this coming kingdom  by changing their ways and devoting  themselves to god and actively engaging  in helping those in need  those who do that  will be fulfilling god's law the torah  and those are the people that god will  enable to enter into his kingdom  at the end of his life jesus decided to  take his message to the capital of the  jewish homeland jerusalem itself  and its glorious temple where many  thousands of jews would be gathered  together  during one of the great jewish feasts  passover  an annual festival that celebrated god's  deliverance of his people israel from  their slavery in egypt many centuries  before as recounted in the book of  exodus  jesus made a journey with his disciples  to jerusalem  and while he was there he proclaimed his  message that there is a coming  destruction and people need to repent to  prepare for it so that they could enter  into the kingdom  this message included some inflammatory  ideas that would not have set well with  the ruling establishment  god was soon going to destroy his  enemies those who are now in power  including the roman overlords  and the priests who are running the  temple who cooperated with the roman  overlords  jesus appears to have acquired enough  listeners  to make those in power nervous  about a riot possibly breaking out over  his incendiary words  during this festival when jerusalem had  multiplied in size with all the pilgrims  and so the authorities had jesus  arrested  jesus appeared before the roman governor  pontius pilate  who was in the city precisely to control  the crowds pilate normally lived in  caesarea but for the passover feast he'd  come in because of the crowds  after a brief trial  on a charge of sedition against the  state  in which jesus was asked  are you the king of the jews  pilate decided to hand him over to be  crucified  jesus was declaring himself the king of  the coming kingdom  that would replace the kingdom of rome  this was a charge of sedition  jesus was immediately taken off to be  crucified  according to the earliest accounts  he was dead within six hours  in the ancient roman world there was no  such thing as due process  the roman governor pilate could decide  jesus fate on the spot  and he did so  jesus then was executed for believing  this apocalyptic message that the end  was coming soon the kingdom of god would  soon arrive  and he would be made the king  this way of looking at jesus life and  teaching may not be exactly what you've  heard before  but it's become a standard view among  scholars of the new testament since  albert schweitzer wrote his classic  study the quest of the historical jesus  well over a century ago where schweitzer  argued that jesus was in fact an  apocalypticist and that this was the  core of his message  in particular you may have noticed that  in this summary i've given i've not said  anything about jesus preaching of his  death and resurrection  that that is what bring would bring  about the salvation of the world  jesus death and resurrection of course  is the central teaching of christianity  it doesn't appear to be what jesus  himself preached  based on a critical study of our  earliest sources  that's why using the historical methods  i described earlier i asked was jesus a  christian  his message was apparently not that his  crucifixion would bring about the  salvation of the world the historical  jesus did not preach about a salvation  based on his death and resurrection he  preached about the need to repent and  return to keeping god's law and view of  the coming kingdom  as scholars of early christianity have  long said  christianity became the religion about  jesus rather than than the religion of  jesus  jesus own religion was the jewish view  that the god of israel was soon to  intervene in the world by sending a  cosmic savior from heaven to destroy the  forces of evil  and set up his kingdom on earth with  jesus himself at the head  after his death  his followers shifted the message  it was the death of jesus and his  resurrection that ultimately mattered  that brought salvation  how this shift actually happened is a  crucial factor in understanding how the  followers of jesus formed a religion  that could take over the  world that will be the subject of the  next lecture
 

